16\pi = 2\pi r \implies r = 8 \text{ inches}
\] The volume \(V\) of a cone is given by the formula \(V = \frac{1}{3}\pi r^2 h\), where \(r\) is the radius and \(h\) is the height. For the original cone, the height \(h_1\) is 30 inches. The volume of the original cone \(V_1\) is: \[
V_1 = \frac{1}{3}\pi (8)^2 (30) = \frac{1}{3}\pi \cdot 64 \cdot 30 = 640\pi \text{ cubic inches}
\] When the height is reduced to \(h_2\) while the circumference stays the same (so the radius \(r\) remains 8 inches), the new volume \(V_2\) is given as \(192\pi\) cubic inches. The new radius \(r\) is still 8 inches. So, we have: \[
V_2 = \frac{1}{3}\pi (8)^2 h_2 = \frac{1}{3}\pi \cdot 64 \cdot h_2 = 192\pi \text{ cubic inches}
\] From the equation above, we can solve for \(h_2\): \[
\frac{1}{3}\pi \cdot 64 \cdot h_2 = 192\pi \implies \frac{64 h_2}{3} = 192 \implies 64 h_2 = 576 \implies h_2 = \frac{576}{64} = 9 \text{ inches}
