 The circumference of the base of the cone is given as \(16\pi\) inches. The circumference of a circle is given by \(2\pi r\), where \(r\) is the radius. So we can find the radius \(r\) as follows: \[
2\pi r = 16\pi \implies r = \frac{16\pi}{2\pi} = 8 \text{ inches}
\] The height of the original cone is \(30\) inches. The volume \(V\) of a cone is given by: \[
V = \frac{1}{3}\pi r^2 h
\] So the volume of the original cone is: \[
V_{\text{original}} = \frac{1}{3}\pi (8)^2 (30) = \frac{1}{3}\pi (64)(30) = 640\pi \text{ cubic inches}
\] The height of the shorter cone is reduced while the circumference stays the same, so the radius remains \(8\) inches. The volume of the shorter cone is given as \(192\pi\) cubic inches. We need to find the height \(h_{\text{short}}\) of the shorter cone. Using the volume formula for the cone: \[
192\pi = \frac{1}{3}\pi (8)^2 h_{\text{short}}
\] Simplifying this equation: \[
192\pi = \frac{1}{3}\pi (64) h_{\text{short}} \implies 192\pi = \frac{64\pi}{3} h_{\text{short}}
\] Solving for \(h_{\text{short}}\): \[
192\pi \cdot \frac{3}{64\pi} = h_{\text{short}} \implies h_{\text{short}} = \frac{192 \cdot 3}{64} = 9 \text{ inches}
\] The ratio of the shorter height to the original height is: \[
\frac{h_{\text{short}}}{h_{\text{original}}} = \frac{9}{30} = \frac{3}{10}
\] <think> The ratio of the shorter height to the original height is \(\boxed{\frac{3}{10}}\). </think> <answer> \frac{3}{10} </answer>
